    "content": "A low-carbon economy aims to minimize greenhouse gas emissions, primarily CO2cap C cap O sub 2𝐶𝑂2, through renewable energy, energy efficiency, and sustainable practices. Key techniques include solar/wind power, Carbon Capture and Storage (CCS), green hydrogen, and eco-conscious construction. These methods facilitate a global transition to meet net-zero goals by 2050. Low-Carbon Economy Techniques Renewable Energy Generation: Utilizing wind, solar, and hydropower to replace fossil fuels. Carbon Capture and Storage (CCS): Capturing and storing CO2cap C cap O sub 2𝐶𝑂2 from industrial processes. Energy Efficiency: Employing smart grids, LEDs, and improved insulation to lower energy demand. Low-Carbon Materials: Developing sustainable alternatives like low-carbon cement. Sustainable Transport & Fuels: Switching to electric vehicles and using biofuels or hydrogen. Low-Carbon Economy Functions Decarbonization: Actively reducing dependence on fossil fuels in high-emission sectors. Environmental Protection: Mitigating global warming by lowering CO2cap C cap O sub 2𝐶𝑂2 intensity. Economic Transition: Creating new jobs, stimulating innovation, and supporting, green energy infrastructure. Energy Security: Diversifying energy sources to reduce reliance on volatile fuel markets. Common Sectors of Application Construction/Architecture: Using low-carbon materials to create sustainable buildings. Energy & Power: Building renewable capacity (solar, wind). Industrial Processes: Adopting CCS for hard-to-abate sectors like steel and cement.",
